  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Named after John Marshall Wilson, who operated a gristmill and cotton gin in the mid‑1800s, Wilson’s Mills blossomed with the arrival of the North Carolina Railroad in 1856.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wilsonsmillsnc.org/about-wilsons-mills"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was officially incorporated on August 2, 1996, though its charter to bring electricity to the mills dates back to 1868.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wilsonsmillsnc.org/about-wilsons-mills"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wilson’s Mills sits 6 miles north of Smithfield, the county seat of Johnston County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilson's_Mills%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Route 70 cuts through the southern edge of Wilson’s Mills, linking it 26 miles northwest to Raleigh and 6 miles southeast to Interstate 95 at Selma.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilson's_Mills%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wilson’s Mills operates under a council‑manager government, with all council members elected at‑large.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilson's_Mills%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Wilson’s Mills Elementary School, the town’s only public school, anchors community life for local families.",
